{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '625,765p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '1158,1255p'\ngit diff -- docs/design/datom.world.md\nrg -n ':global|seq/list|injectiv|print.var|print binding|unbound|not implemented|unverified|as-is|whole.*grammar|row-only|blocked|blocking' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '625,765p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '1158,1255p'\ngit diff -- docs/design/datom.world.md\nrg -n ':global|seq/list|injectiv|print.var|print binding|unbound|not implemented|unverified|as-is|whole.*grammar|row-only|blocked|blocking' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"   625\tsites reference this lambda's content\" is a join on its address (§6.4).\n   626\tBecause this design retains named variables in the canonical tuple (§2, §5.1),\n   627\tthis structural sharing requires exact identity, not alpha-equivalence:\n   628\ttwo functions that are identical up to variable renaming project to\n   629\t*different* rows, since the parameter and variable names are part of the\n   630\thashed content. Only subtrees identical in structure *and* in every retained\n   631\tname collapse to one row. The row set holds each such distinct subtree once,\n   632\tand parent slots point at it — while identity stays per row.\n   633\t**This structural collapse depends on the §4.2 encoder being injective, which it now is for ordinary values under default print bindings.** The three conformance pairs (vector/list, vector/seq, collection metadata) that used to make the encoder non-injective are closed; structurally distinct ordinary values no longer hash equal under the default host print configuration. The disclosed residuals remain gaps: scalar metadata and byte-array identity hashing need a future producer to matter here, but ambient print-var bindings reaching scalar bytes are live now — a row whose slots include scalars hashed inside such a binding is not covered by this collapse guarantee — see §4.2.\n   634\t`:eid` is dropped from the frontends and the codec.\n   635\t\n   636\tWhat `:eid` also did, and content addresses do not, is name a *place*.\n   637\t\"This call site\" is an occurrence key `[origin root-address path]` (§2.5),\n   638\tnot the site's content address; a query that needs distinct call sites\n   639\tjoins the occurrence relation, not subtree addresses, and a provenance\n   640\tlink that must survive a second identical site, a second identical file,\n   641\tor a second identical expansion records the occurrence key. Content\n   642\taddresses serve sharing and cross-media correspondence of content;\n   643\toccurrence keys serve identity of places, sources, and attempts.\n   644\t\n   645\t### 4.5 Free variables are queried, not tagged\n   646\t\n   647\tAn earlier revision of this design split variable references into two\n   648\tnode types: `:variable` for names bound by an enclosing `:lambda`'s\n   649\tparams, resolved against the lexical environment only, and `:global` for\n   650\tfree names, resolved through `resolve-var`'s store → primitives →\n   651\tmodules fallthrough. The motivation was static inspectability: knowing a\n   652\tprogram's free-name dependencies (for UCF §7.6.1's `:yin.k/requires`\n   653\tfixed point, and for capability checking before running untrusted\n   654\tcontent) without evaluating it.\n   655\t\n   656\tThat motivation does not require a second tag. Whether a `:variable` row\n   657\tis free or bound is exactly what a **query** over `$ast` (§6.1) can\n   658\tcompute: walk the row's ancestor chain (a `node`/`nodes` slot pointing at\n   659\ta row's own id is a parent-child edge, so the ancestor relation is a\n   660\trecursive Datalog rule over the row relation itself, needing no separate\n   661\toccurrence side-table) and check whether the `:variable`'s `name` appears\n   662\tin any enclosing `:lambda`'s `params`. If it does not, the name is free —\n   663\ta `:global`-shaped fact, derived on demand, never persisted. `dao.space.query/q`\n   664\talready supports recursive rules (`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`'s\n   665\t`eval-rule`), so this is not a capability gap; it is a query the design\n   666\tnever needed to avoid.\n   667\t\n   668\tSplitting the tag instead of querying for the same fact bought nothing\n   669\tthe query could not already give, and cost real things: the name\n   670\tcollided with the project's own \"no hidden global state\" invariant — the\n   671\t`store` a `:global` reference resolves into is genuinely mutable and\n   672\ttime-varying (it holds `yin/def` results alongside stream handles and\n   673\tcursor entries, §7.7.2), so the naming confusion was a real signal, not\n   674\tcosmetic; the no-fallthrough contract (`:variable`'s lexical-only\n   675\tresolution, evaluating an unbound name to `nil` rather than falling\n   676\tthrough to store/primitives/modules) replaced a forgiving runtime\n   677\tfallthrough with a requirement that every frontend get free/bound\n   678\tclassification exactly right, which is precisely the hardest part of\n   679\twriting a correctly hygienic macro expander; and the change depended on\n   680\tfrontend scope-analysis work (classifying free names as `:global` at\n   681\tauthoring time) that does not exist anywhere in this codebase. This is\n   682\tnow a decided case study for the broader principle in\n   683\t[`docs/design/datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md)'s Design Principles:\n   684\tbefore adding structure to a canonical, content-hashed representation,\n   685\tcheck whether a query already gives you the fact for free.\n   686\t\n   687\tSee `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc` for a working demonstration: a\n   688\trecursive rule (`edge`/`anc`/`depth`/`bound?`, verified against the real\n   689\t`dao.space.query/q` engine, not sketched) computing the free-name set of\n   690\ta row tree with no `:global` tag anywhere in it, including the\n   691\tnearest-enclosing-binder case under shadowing. `edge`'s clauses are\n   692\thardcoded for the fixture's two tags (`:lambda`'s body slot,\n   693\t`:application`'s operator/operands slots), not generic over §2.3's full\n   694\tgrammar — a tree using a tag with other node-valued slots (`:if`,\n   695\t`:dao.stream.apply/call`, `:vm/resume`, the `:stream/*` family) has\n   696\tedges this specific rule does not walk, so it can call a bound name free\n   697\t(an overcount, not an undercount — still conservative-safe for §7.6.1,\n   698\tjust imprecise). A production rule needs an `edge` clause per node-valued\n   699\tslot in the grammar, or a schema-driven walk over\n   700\t`semantic-bytecode-grammar` instead of one clause per tag.\n   701\t\n   702\t**The row-only query is unsound, not just imprecise, when a name is free\n   703\tat one occurrence and bound at another — and this is not a permanent\n   704\tcost of dropping `:global`, only of the simple query.** Because\n   705\tstructurally identical subtrees share one row (§4.4), a name that is\n   706\tfree at one occurrence and bound at another (e.g. `((fn [x] x) x)`, whose\n   707\toperand `x` is free while the lambda body's `x` is bound) collapses to\n   708\tone `:variable` row with multiple parent edges — one through the binding\n   709\tlambda, one not. `bound?`'s row-level walk (`test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`)\n   710\tfinds a binding ancestor via *either* edge and calls the whole row bound,\n   711\twhich would silently drop the genuinely free occurrence from a computed\n   712\t`:yin.k/requires` set. UCF §7.6.1 requires that set to be a **conservative**\n   713\tfixed point — it must never undercount what a program depends on, since\n   714\tundercounting a capability requirement is a security defect, not an\n   715\timprecision. The row-only query can undercount in this mixed case, so it\n   716\tis not sufficient for `:yin.k/requires` on its own.\n   717\t\n   718\tThe fix is not to bring `:global` back. A name's free/bound status at a\n   719\t*specific occurrence* is exactly a places-not-contents question, and\n   720\t§6.1 already routes those through the occurrence relation\n   721\t(`[origin root-address path ...]`, §2.5) rather than row identity: walk\n   722\teach occurrence's own path to its ancestors, not the row's abstracted\n   723\tparent edges. This is not merely argued — `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`'s\n   724\t`occurrence-aware-rules-resolve-mixed-free-and-bound-rows` proves it\n   725\tagainst the real `q` engine, on the exact `((fn [x] x) x)` case above: a\n   726\thand-built occurrence relation of §2.5's shape (the production indexer\n   727\tthat would emit it does not exist yet, so the test builds it as fixture\n   728\tdata, same honest scoping as the row-level demo), a recursive rule\n   729\twalking path-prefix ancestry rather than row edges, and — the point of\n   730\tthe exercise — a **contrast assertion that runs the row-only rule against\n   731\tthe same database and confirms it actually does undercount** (`#{}`\n   732\tinstead of `#{'x}`), so the failure mode §4.5 describes above is\n   733\tdemonstrated, not narrated. No new structure was needed; only the\n   734\tricher, occurrence-joined query, exactly as this section claims.\n   735\t\n   736\t**Not root-scoped as written — a real gap, not a hedge.** The test's\n   737\t`occ-anc`/`occ-bound?` rules take only `?path` and `?name` as arguments;\n   738\tthey never thread a root through the recursion. Over a single tree this\n   739\tis harmless, but a production occurrence relation holds every reachable\n   740\ttree's occurrences together, and structural paths are not\n   741\troot-qualified — two different trees can produce the identical literal\n   742\tpath (e.g. both have a lambda at path `[2]`). `occ-anc`/`occ-bound?` as\n   743\twritten would then find a binder in the WRONG tree and misclassify an\n   744\toccurrence in one tree against a binder in another. Whoever wires up the\n   745\treal `:yin.k/requires` computation (open item 5, \"Conservative dependency\n   746\tcompletion\") must thread `?root` through every rule head\n   747\t(`p-up`/`occ-anc`/`occ-bound?`) and constrain the `[$occ ...]` join to\n   748\tone root-address, not lift the test's rule set unmodified. What the test\n   749\tactually exercises is one single-root fixture using `:lambda` and\n   750\t`:application` only — it does not test `:if` or any other node-valued\n   751\ttag. What it establishes beyond that fixture is reasoning, not tested\n   752\tcoverage: the occurrence rule walks path prefixes generically rather\n   753\tthan one edge clause per tag, so — unlike the row-only rule's own\n   754\ttag-coverage gap noted below — nothing about its approach depends on\n   755\twhich tag sits at a given path, which is why it should generalize across\n   756\tthe whole §2.3 grammar without the row-only rule's gap. That reasoning is\n   757\tnot itself tested here. What remains before production use is\n   758\troot-scoping the rules and building the indexer that emits occurrence\n   759\ttuples in the first place — both implementation work, not open design\n   760\tquestions.\n   761\t\n   762\t---\n   763\t\n   764\t## §5 The segment level\n   765\t\n  1158\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1159\t| `:target-bounds` | a `:jump`/`:branch-false` target or a `:closure` body is not an integer in `[0, length)`                                                            |\n  1160\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1161\t| `:terminator`    | the last instruction is not in `code/terminators` (`code.cljc:19-21`)                                                                               |\n  1162\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1163\t| `:argc`          | a `:call`/`:ffi-call` argc is not a non-negative integer                                                                                            |\n  1164\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1165\t\n  1166\tThere is no vector-level binding rule for `:var`: nesting is a property of the AST. `resolve-var`'s retained fallthrough (env → store → primitives → modules, §4.5, §7.7.2) throws if nothing resolves (`engine.cljc:46-65`) — a `:var` name is validated only as a symbol here (§7.5's `:operand-kind`), never checked for boundedness at this level.\n  1167\t\n  1168\tThe address check (the vector hashes to the address it claims) is UCF\n  1169\t§7.3.4's and runs before these rules whenever an address is claimed; a\n  1170\tcorrect hash is never structural validation. The failure outcome of both\n  1171\tcheckers on the UCF lowering path is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc or\n  1172\tpath, as §7.3.4 already specifies; on a local load it is a load error\n  1173\tnaming the same.\n  1174\t\n  1175\t### 7.6 The hot loop never queries\n  1176\t\n  1177\tThe walker dispatches on `(:type node)` and reads named fields of the map\n  1178\tAST it reconstructed at load; the semantic VM runs `(aget code pc)` over\n  1179\tan image decoded once at load (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.1). Neither touches\n  1180\ta relation, a ref, an address\n  1181\tindex, or a projection while stepping. Projection and query are load-time\n  1182\tand tooling-time operations. This is the rule that keeps\n  1183\t`yin.vm.semantic.md` §6's cost model intact under this design: queryability\n  1184\tis paid once at load, or by whoever asks, never per step.\n  1185\t\n  1186\t### 7.7 Dependency closure is Datalog over the tuples\n  1187\t\n  1188\tUCF §7.6.1 computes `:yin.k/requires` by a conservative fixed point over\n  1189\tvalues, code, names, and modules. The **code part** of that fixed point is\n  1190\tthe queries below, run over the flat rows of every reachable tree\n  1191\tand the segment-qualified rows (§6.2) of every reachable segment. The\n  1192\tvalue, name-satisfaction, and module parts remain the walk §7.6.1\n  1193\tspecifies; these queries supply their inputs and do not replace them.\n  1194\t\n  1195\tExtraction over trees (`$ast` is the union of flat rows of the reachable\n  1196\ttrees):\n  1197\t\n  1198\t```clojure\n  1199\t;; Free-name extraction is a query, not a tag match (§4.5): a :variable\n  1200\t;; occurrence's name is a free-name obligation unless it is bound by an\n  1201\t;; enclosing :lambda on ITS OWN occurrence path. This must be an\n  1202\t;; occurrence-relation query (§2.5's [origin root-address path]), not a\n  1203\t;; row-only one: content-addressed sharing means one :variable row can\n  1204\t;; have several occurrences, and a row-only rule that walks row parent\n  1205\t;; edges instead of per-occurrence paths can find a binder through one\n  1206\t;; occurrence's edge and wrongly call every occurrence of that row bound\n  1207\t;; — undercounting a real dependency, which UCF §7.6.1's conservative\n  1208\t;; fixed point must never do (§4.5). See `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`'s\n  1209\t;; occurrence-aware rule set for the verified version (scoped per root —\n  1210\t;; a production query must additionally constrain every rule to one\n  1211\t;; root-address, since an unscoped rule set can otherwise resolve a\n  1212\t;; free name in one tree against a binder in a different reachable\n  1213\t;; tree); the shape here is illustrative, not the exact syntax.\n  1214\t[:find ?name :in $ast $occ % ?root\n  1215\t :where [$occ ?root ?path ?v] [?v :variable ?name] (not (bound? ?root ?path ?name))]\n  1216\t\n  1217\t;; store keys read or written by code\n  1218\t[:find ?key :in $ast :where (or [$ast _ :vm/store-get ?key]\n  1219\t                                [$ast _ :vm/store-put ?key _])]\n  1220\t\n  1221\t;; FFI ops\n  1222\t[:find ?op :in $ast :where [$ast _ :dao.stream.apply/call ?op _]]\n  1223\t\n  1224\t;; parked ids named by resume\n  1225\t[:find ?pid :in $ast :where [$ast _ :vm/resume ?pid _]]\n  1226\t\n  1227\t;; syntax that raises an effect: the tag, to be normalized by the footprint table below\n  1228\t[:find ?tag :in $ast\n  1229\t :where [$ast _ ?tag & _]\n  1230\t        [(contains? #{:stream/make :stream/put :stream/cursor :stream/next\n  1231\t                      :stream/close :dao.stream.apply/call} ?tag)]]\n  1232\t```\n  1233\t\n  1234\tExtraction over segments (`$code` is the union of segment-qualified rows):\n  1235\t\n  1236\t```clojure\n  1237\t;; likewise over segment-qualified rows: derive free names from the\n  1238\t;; instruction vector's own scoping, no separate opcode needed. Unlike\n  1239\t;; AST rows, instructions are positionally addressed within one segment\n  1240\t;; (segment-address, pc), not content-shared sub-vector by sub-vector, so\n  1241\t;; the mixed-occurrence risk §4.5 documents for AST rows may not apply\n  1242\t;; here the same way — this is unverified. `linearize.cljc/lower` exists\n  1243\t;; today (`:162`) but takes `[e a v t m]` datoms, not the row sets this\n  1244\t;; design specifies (§9.1: lower must be adapted to read the row relation\n  1245\t;; instead); no test exercises this query against the row-set-based\n  1246\t;; lowering, so confirm this reasoning when that adaptation lands rather\n  1247\t;; than assuming it.\n  1248\t[:find ?name :in $code % :where [$code _ _ :var ?name] (not (bound? ?name))]\n  1249\t[:find ?key  :in $code :where (or [$code _ _ :store-get ?key]\n  1250\t                                  [$code _ _ :store-put ?key _])]\n  1251\t[:find ?op   :in $code :where [$code _ _ :ffi-call ?op _]]\n  1252\t[:find ?pid  :in $code :where [$code _ _ :resume ?pid]]\n  1253\t[:find ?mn   :in $code\n  1254\t :where [$code _ _ ?mn & _]\n  1255\t        [(contains? #{:stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next\ndiff --git a/docs/design/datom.world.md b/docs/design/datom.world.md\nindex ddd6385..b3c2a25 100644\n--- a/docs/design/datom.world.md\n+++ b/docs/design/datom.world.md\n@@ -138,6 +138,28 @@ result. That is a correct outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n Code quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\n Stigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n \n+**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a new tag, slot, or field to any\n+canonical, content-hashed tuple structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema,\n+a row shape), check whether the fact it would encode is instead computable\n+from the existing structure via a Datalog query over `dao.space.query/q`,\n+at the correct scope for what the fact actually depends on — not every\n+query over the shallowest available relation is sound; get the scope\n+wrong and a query can silently mis-derive the fact instead of failing\n+loudly. If a correctly-scoped query can derive it, it belongs as a query\n+or a projection, not as new persisted structure: new structure is\n+content-hashed and migrates every downstream consumer, and duplicates\n+authority over a fact a query could derive fresh instead. This is not a\n+zero-cost trade — deriving still costs real query complexity and, if the\n+fact isn't already indexed, the work of building and maintaining whatever\n+does index it — but that cost is paid once, in the query and its index,\n+not in every future migration of the persisted structure. See\n+[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md)'s\n+retired `:global` tag for the concrete case study — a free/bound variable\n+distinction that looked like it needed a new AST node, until it turned\n+out to be computable by query; getting the scope right there took two\n+rounds and a real correctness bug (a query that looked sufficient\n+undercounted a dependency in a case the simpler query didn't cover).\n+\n See [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n See [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n \n156:The fields are what the walker dispatches on today (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:333-470`); the `:lambda` arm already binds `params` by name, and the `:variable` arm already resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (`ast_walker.cljc:361-363`; the semantic VM's `:var` opcode does the same at `semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this design keeps that fallthrough rather than splitting free and bound references into separate node types (§4.5). Separately, closure application (`ast_walker.cljc:191`, and its hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`; `semantic.cljc:200`) still binds arguments with `(zipmap params args)`, which leaves missing parameter names unbound rather than bound to `nil` — that binding change belongs to the application arm and the semantic VM's apply, not to `:lambda`.\n555:### 4.2 The transitional encoder is inherited; ordinary identity use is unblocked\n559:conformance pairs this section used to name as blocking collisions are\n577:rather than content, and ambient print-var bindings still reach scalar\n589:those specific value shapes before they matter, while ambient print-var\n633:**This structural collapse depends on the §4.2 encoder being injective, which it now is for ordinary values under default print bindings.** The three conformance pairs (vector/list, vector/seq, collection metadata) that used to make the encoder non-injective are closed; structurally distinct ordinary values no longer hash equal under the default host print configuration. The disclosed residuals remain gaps: scalar metadata and byte-array identity hashing need a future producer to matter here, but ambient print-var bindings reaching scalar bytes are live now — a row whose slots include scalars hashed inside such a binding is not covered by this collapse guarantee — see §4.2.\n649:params, resolved against the lexical environment only, and `:global` for\n663:a `:global`-shaped fact, derived on demand, never persisted. `dao.space.query/q`\n671:`store` a `:global` reference resolves into is genuinely mutable and\n675:resolution, evaluating an unbound name to `nil` rather than falling\n680:frontend scope-analysis work (classifying free names as `:global` at\n690:a row tree with no `:global` tag anywhere in it, including the\n702:**The row-only query is unsound, not just imprecise, when a name is free\n704:cost of dropping `:global`, only of the simple query.** Because\n715:imprecision. The row-only query can undercount in this mixed case, so it\n718:The fix is not to bring `:global` back. A name's free/bound status at a\n730:the exercise — a **contrast assertion that runs the row-only rule against\n753:than one edge clause per tag, so — unlike the row-only rule's own\n756:the whole §2.3 grammar without the row-only rule's gap. That reasoning is\n913:for ordinary values it is no longer blocked (§4.2). The occurrence\n1011:| attribute-dimension queries with the attribute unbound, `[?e ?a ?v]` | a flat row has no attribute slot; \"every fact about this node\" is an EAV question               |\n1135:restricted admission domain, and ordinary identity uses are unblocked.\n1203:;; row-only one: content-addressed sharing means one :variable row can\n1204:;; have several occurrences, and a row-only rule that walks row parent\n1242:;; here the same way — this is unverified. `linearize.cljc/lower` exists\n1351:**Under-arity calls leave missing parameters bound to `nil`, not unbound.**\n1416:footprint was declared. It is `:blocked` when an address cannot be\n1948:**unverified**:\n1991:   inherited, still open, but **no longer blocking ordinary identity and\n1993:   this item used to name as blocking (vector/list, vector/seq,\n1999:   ambient print-var bindings on scalars — `dao.jing.md`'s Open Items)\n2044:   row-only one, to stay conservative when a name is free at one\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
