{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'global|lexical|free.name|bound|scope|conformance pairs|encoder|print|derive|persist|cost' docs/design/datom.world.md\nrg -n 'environment|resolv|params|De Bruijn|positional|scope|proof|proves|verified|guarantee' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"16:1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams. Persistent facts are immutable tuples and datoms `[e a v t m]`.\n23:- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n33:A datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata).\n34:Source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly. Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs.\n41:No direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n63:### Host Boundaries\n65:Host-specific boundaries are isolated with DaoStream and interpreters of\n66:DaoStream. A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call.\n69:with that boundary: their exhaustive outcome maps are stream protocol, not an\n97:deposit channel fails, it may close that resource to make the boundary\n105:not need to. This is what keeps the layering one-way: a boundary depends on\n108:A transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n130:Host error types never cross the boundary. A transform function classifies them\n141:**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a new tag, slot, or field to any\n145:at the correct scope for what the fact actually depends on — not every\n146:query over the shallowest available relation is sound; get the scope\n147:wrong and a query can silently mis-derive the fact instead of failing\n148:loudly. If a correctly-scoped query can derive it, it belongs as a query\n149:or a projection, not as new persisted structure: new structure is\n151:authority over a fact a query could derive fresh instead. This is not a\n152:zero-cost trade — deriving still costs real query complexity and, if the\n154:does index it — but that cost is paid once, in the query and its index,\n155:not in every future migration of the persisted structure. See\n157:retired `:global` tag for the concrete case study — a free/bound variable\n159:out to be computable by query; getting the scope right there took two\n163:See [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n28:[`dao.space.query.md`](./dao.space.query.md) (the positional Datalog\n32:re-verified on 2026-09-15.\n45:| **Content**    | `{:type :lambda :params [x]…}`,| The Map AST code itself, stored as  | the content        | `dao.jing`, under its    | immutable; no `t`, no `m`, no  |\n149: :params [x],\n156:The fields are what the walker dispatches on today (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:333-470`); the `:lambda` arm already binds `params` by name, and the `:variable` arm already resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (`ast_walker.cljc:361-363`; the semantic VM's `:var` opcode does the same at `semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this design keeps that fallthrough rather than splitting free and bound references into separate node types (§4.5). Separately, closure application (`ast_walker.cljc:191`, and its hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`; `semantic.cljc:200`) still binds arguments with `(zipmap params args)`, which leaves missing parameter names unbound rather than bound to `nil` — that binding change belongs to the application arm and the semantic VM's apply, not to `:lambda`.\n273:| :lambda                 | 3          | [:lambda params body]                | params body              | syms, node       | unchanged: `ast_walker.cljc:364-370` | unchanged: `v2.cljc:419-424` |\n305:**Named Variables:** A `:variable` row's `name` retains the original symbol from the AST. (De Bruijn computation is deferred to the Register VM phase).\n363:  projection retains them in the canonical tuples (`:lambda params`,\n476:primitive symbol resolved and profile-checked per UCF §7.5.2, is not\n486:- `yin/def` and `f` resolve through `resolve-var`'s precedence, store\n488:  rewrite is equivalent only when neither `yin/def` nor `f` is shadowed by an enclosing `:lambda`'s params at the site (so both resolve as free names, through the fallthrough, rather than to a local binding);\n584:and `dao.jing` reads that resolve an address to a literal value may treat\n586:pre-existing, scoped, and tracked as `dao.jing.md` Open Items rather than\n620:call-site operator resolve to one entity so provenance can point at it.\n633:**This structural collapse depends on the §4.2 encoder being injective, which it now is for ordinary values under default print bindings.** The three conformance pairs (vector/list, vector/seq, collection metadata) that used to make the encoder non-injective are closed; structurally distinct ordinary values no longer hash equal under the default host print configuration. The disclosed residuals remain gaps: scalar metadata and byte-array identity hashing need a future producer to matter here, but ambient print-var bindings reaching scalar bytes are live now — a row whose slots include scalars hashed inside such a binding is not covered by this collapse guarantee — see §4.2.\n649:params, resolved against the lexical environment only, and `:global` for\n650:free names, resolved through `resolve-var`'s store → primitives →\n662:in any enclosing `:lambda`'s `params`. If it does not, the name is free —\n671:`store` a `:global` reference resolves into is genuinely mutable and\n680:frontend scope-analysis work (classifying free names as `:global` at\n688:recursive rule (`edge`/`anc`/`depth`/`bound?`, verified against the real\n724:`occurrence-aware-rules-resolve-mixed-free-and-bound-rows` proves it\n736:**Not root-scoped as written — a real gap, not a hedge.** The test's\n768:The segment's canonical form is the positional instruction tuple vector of\n771:kinds, defaults are saturated, refs are resolved pcs, and the header folds\n775:tuple are both flat positional tuples, canonical and content-addressed,\n810:Its declared scope does not include the AST-to-instruction algorithm, and\n845:1. **Content integrity.** The consumer verifies the claimed root ID against the root row and verifies each required row as `row.id == segment-key(row-body)`, recursively validating the reachable closure before reconstructing the semantic map. The instruction vector is verified similarly. Failure is\n887:them directly — ruling 3 of 2026-09-14 was verified live over exactly\n901:exclusions, saturate per §2.4, positionalize per the §2.3 dictionary,\n933:and source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to a tuple slot\n945:positional unification: `eval-pattern-clause` (`query.cljc:894-907`) parses\n996:`$refs` is a `current` view (§8.6), so the name resolves to its current\n1039:| semantic             | instruction vector   | validate (§7.5), decode positional operands into the image (the `case` of `semantic.cljc:563-593` reading `(nth tuple i)`|\n1119:| `:id-resolves`    | a child id in a `node` or `nodes` slot names no row of the loaded set                                                                             |\n1127:resolve within the loaded set and close under the root, as a jump target\n1140:dense pcs, sorted by pc, refs resolve to entities) are satisfied by the\n1154:|                  | a `:var` name is not a symbol, a `:closure` params is not a vector of symbols, a `:ffi-call` op or `:resume` parked id is not a keyword, a `:gensym`  |\n1166:There is no vector-level binding rule for `:var`: nesting is a property of the AST. `resolve-var`'s retained fallthrough (env → store → primitives → modules, §4.5, §7.7.2) throws if nothing resolves (`engine.cljc:46-65`) — a `:var` name is validated only as a symbol here (§7.5's `:operand-kind`), never checked for boundedness at this level.\n1209:;; occurrence-aware rule set for the verified version (scoped per root —\n1211:;; root-address, since an unscoped rule set can otherwise resolve a\n1239:;; AST rows, instructions are positionally addressed within one segment\n1242:;; here the same way — this is unverified. `linearize.cljc/lower` exists\n1338:#### 7.7.2 Name obligations are resolved per store slice, and completion is conservative\n1344:every `:variable` name through `resolve-var`'s existing precedence, env →\n1353:over-arity call drops extra arguments beyond the params list length. This\n1357:absent from the extended environment rather than explicitly `nil` — so it\n1359:environment (or, transitively, the store/primitives/modules chain) has\n1363:application, not about resolving a name to a value.\n1375:sentence that a name \"is satisfied if it is bound in any environment the\n1384:environments are two work items, because each enters the segment with its\n1385:own environment (`semantic.cljc:199-205`) and so reaches different values (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), which\n1387:closure's captured environment, and nothing else. This context\n1415:as a profiled primitive or module requirement, every parked id resolved, and every module\n1483:|            | path :yin.ledger/origin [:source medium batch j]`     | `:yin.ledger/attempt` is the portable attempt identity of §8.4.1, scoped to the expander          |\n1605:enforced by how the transactor resolves ids: `apply-tempid-map` replaces a\n1613:  `:yin/source-batch`, which no transactor resolves, as today;\n1617:  parent event's id, the successor of `:yin/source-node` and resolved by\n1642:#### 8.4.1 Attempt identity is scoped to an expander incarnation\n1670:  are already resolved (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:231-254`);\n1744:  resolved batch; nothing requires entity order to equal structural\n1818:  declaration whose coordinates do not resolve to such a node is an\n1854:which resolves the greatest `t` per `[e a v]` and then removes retractions\n1903:| walker, `ast_walker.cljc`                        | Retains named variable evaluation (`:name`). `:lambda` arm binds by name. Does not use De Bruijn numbering. `:variable` arm (`:361-363`) is unchanged — still resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (§4.5). Closure application (`:191`, hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`) changes from `(zipmap params args)` to nil-filling missing params. Adds `:stream/close` arm (§3.2).                                                                                                      |\n1910:| `:230-242`                                       | `lower` takes a row set and returns a vector plus the §5.3 provenance table; emits `:var name` and `:closure params body`; `lower-ast` (`:245-252`) goes away     |\n1913:|                                                  | `:yin/root` and `:eid` removed; `:yin/address` added; retains `:yin/params` on lambda and `:yin/name` on variable |\n1918:| `semantic/load-image`,                           | decodes from the vector directly on the primary path; frame binding for `:closure` matches arguments to `params` by position, binding each to its name (§7.7.2); apply (`semantic.cljc:200`) changes from `zipmap` to nil-filling missing params; `:var` opcode is unchanged, resolving env → store → primitives → modules (§4.5); decodes the new operand (`:closure params body`); both paths call the §7.5 validator   |\n1938:### 9.2 The observer lane: an integration item to scope, not a solved detail\n1948:**unverified**:\n1967:Both are integration items to scope in the implementation phase.\n2039:   store slice by key or resolving via primitive/module profiles (§7.7.2);\n2041:   captured-environment context abstraction for values. Neither analysis exists in code;\n2052:   no derivation can be verified, only content integrity (§5.2.2).\n2054:   any-carried-environment satisfaction sentence is unsound and must be\n2055:   replaced by the store-slice-by-key / profile rule of §7.7.2 (an environment never\n2062:   verified one.\n2088:    the argument-to-named-parameter binding rule of §7.7.2 (arguments match `params` by position, each bound to its name; under-arity call → missing parameter names bound to `nil`; extra arguments beyond the params list length are dropped)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '46,65p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\nsed -n '162,180p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc\nsed -n '1248,1268p' test/dao/space/query_test.cljc\nsed -n '65,74p' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc\nsed -n '1987,2004p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"(defn resolve-var\n  \"Look up a variable name: env -> store -> primitives -> module registry.\n\n   The registry is a value supplied by the composition, not a global.\"\n  [env store primitives registry name]\n  (if-let [pair (find env name)]\n    (val pair)\n    (if-let [pair (find store name)]\n      (val pair)\n      (if-let [pair (find primitives name)]\n        (val pair)\n        (if-let [resolved (when (namespace name)\n                            (module/resolve-module\n                              registry\n                              (symbol (str (namespace name)\n                                           \".\"\n                                           (clojure.core/name name)))))]\n          resolved\n          (fail (str \"Unable to resolve symbol: \" name \" in this context\")\n                {:symbol name}))))))\n(defn lower\n  \"Lower one AST program, as `[e a v t m]` datoms, to one code segment.\n\n   Options:\n     :t        transaction id (default 0)\n     :id-start the segment tempid; instruction pc n is `id-start - 1 - n`.\n               Defaults to below every entity id in the input, so the code\n               and its AST can travel in one batch without colliding.\n\n   Throws naming the node on an unsupported, unknown, or missing node type,\n   or on a host value (a function or object) in a data operand or its\n   metadata. Throws on a supplied :t that is not an integer, and on an\n   :id-start that is not a negative integer, whose range leaves the\n   host-safe integers, or whose range collides with an input entity.\"\n  ([datoms] (lower datoms {}))\n  ([datoms opts]\n   (let [datoms (vec datoms)\n         {:keys [get-attr root-id error]} (vm/index-datoms datoms)\n         _ (when error\n(def ^:private occurrence-rules\n  \"Path-prefix ancestors and per-occurrence bound-ness. [$occ ...] names\n   the occurrence source inside rule bodies (rule seeds carry every :in\n   db); [$occ ?r ?lam-path ?lam] joins an ancestor occurrence's path to\n   its row so the row relation supplies the :lambda shape.\"\n  '[[(p-up ?child ?parent) [(path-pop ?child) ?parent]]\n    [(occ-anc ?a ?d) (p-up ?d ?a)]\n    [(occ-anc ?a ?d) (p-up ?d ?m) (occ-anc ?a ?m)]\n    [(occ-bound? ?path ?name)\n     (occ-anc ?lam-path ?path)\n     [$occ ?r ?lam-path ?lam]\n     [?lam :lambda ?params _]\n     [(member? ?params ?name)]]])\n\n(deftest occurrence-aware-rules-resolve-mixed-free-and-bound-rows\n  ;; ((fn [x] x) x) — the lambda body's x is bound, the operand x is free,\n  ;; and both are the SAME :variable node in the map AST.\n  (let [ast {:type :application,\n             :operator {:type :lambda, :params ['x],\n                        :body {:type :variable, :name 'x}},\n             :operands [{:type :variable, :name 'x}]}\n          (set? n)\n          (prefixed\n            (str \"#{\" (str/join \" \" (map canonical-print n)) \"}\"))\n          (vector? n)\n          (prefixed\n            (str \"[\" (str/join \" \" (map canonical-print n)) \"]\"))\n          (sequential? n)\n          (prefixed\n            (str \"(\" (str/join \" \" (map canonical-print n)) \")\"))\n          :else (pr-str n))))\n\n## §10 Acceptance blockers and open items\n\n1. **The pinned canonical byte encoding in `dao.jing`** — declared and\n   inherited, still open, but **no longer blocking ordinary identity and\n   deduplication use** (§4.2). The three structural conformance pairs\n   this item used to name as blocking (vector/list, vector/seq,\n   collection metadata) are closed by `dao.jing`'s committed encoder fix\n   — list and seq intentionally remain one address, since `=` calls them\n   equal. What\n   remains is the transitional hand printer's disclosed residuals\n   (scalar metadata, pathological symbols, byte-array identity hashing,\n   ambient print-var bindings on scalars — `dao.jing.md`'s Open Items)\n   and the eventual pinned byte encoding itself, neither of which this\n   design closes.\n2. **The store-key domain is the `data` domain** (§2.2): every validator,\n   extraction query, and store-slice encoding must use that one\n   definition. This document now states it uniformly; the implementation\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"## Verdict: **Yes — safe to commit as-is**\n\nAll four r2 findings are resolved:\n\n| Fix | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Runtime variable resolution | **PASS** — [line 1166](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1166) correctly describes fallthrough and throwing when unresolved; the obsolete §7.4 reference is removed. |\n| Encoder sharing guarantee | **PASS** — [line 633](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:633) names vector/seq correctly and qualifies the guarantee by default print bindings and disclosed residuals. |\n| Existing versus proposed lowering | **PASS** — [lines 1242–1247](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1242) distinguish existing datom-based `lower` from the proposed row-set adaptation. |\n| Test coverage versus reasoning | **PASS** — [lines 749–759](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:749) explicitly separate the single-root fixture from the argument for broader applicability. |\n\nThe final sweep found no further blocking issues within this review’s scope. All seven `:global` references remain inside §4.5’s historical discussion; no `seq/list` mislabel remains. The root-scoping caveat accurately preserves the single-root test’s validity without claiming production readiness. The revised principle in `datom.world.md` remains consistent with that distinction.\n\nRead-only review; no files changed and no tests executed."}}
